a fascinating legal issue has come up  again in the derek chalvin trial  specifically with respect to third  degree murder now we've talked about  this in the past  on this channel third degree murder is  not as severe as second or first degree  murder where you're doing something that  is clearly going to injure other people  so it wasn't a surprise that prosecutors  charged shalvin with that particular  crime but there was language  in the minnesota statute that made it  seem like you had to direct that sort of  conduct towards multiple people you  couldn't direct that kind of conduct  towards one particular person well the  minnesota court of appeals stepped in  and said no that's not the case you can  actually direct it towards one  particular person and still be guilty of  third degree murder  andrew from opening arguments was right  he was leading the charge it said the  interpretation of this law that said you  couldn't direct it towards one person  didn't make any sense the minnesota  supreme court could still step in and  reverse the  court of appeals but it does appear that  chalvin was correctly charged with  third-degree murder and the jury will go  forward deliberating  on that particular charge go listen to  one of my favorite legal podcast opening  arguments or i will see you  in court
 

